// WQL — A NON-NUMERIC AGGREGATE ARGUMENT (`min` over a `bool` column).
//
// The third ground of the same refusal: the value does not merely fail to be
// carried into the accumulator, there is no conversion into it in the first
// place. `sum`/`min`/`max` fold in the EL's i64 (or f64 for a float argument)
// and a `bool` — or a `str`, or a `String` — has no route there.
//
// MEASURED at the parent commit (6440e565), this file failed as a host type
// error inside a generated line (`let '__v': type mismatch — expected i64, got
// bool`), i.e. the same anti-pattern as the f32 and u64 cells: a diagnostic
// naming a chunk line and not the clause.
//
// ⚠ THE THREE GROUNDS ARE ONE CHECK, NOT THREE. `check_agg_operand` asks ONE
// question — does the argument's EMITTED type reach the accumulator's type
// (`el_agg_arg_reaches`) — and only the WORDING of the ground branches, on facts
// the lattice already carries (`ElTy.cls`, `ElTy.fits`). A per-type list here
// is exactly what the lattice replaced.
//
// ⚠ THE QUESTION MOVED ON 2026-08-05 AND THIS CELL DID NOT, WHICH IS THE
// CONTROL. It used to be `el_host_carries` — "does the HOST widen this into the
// accumulator with no written cast" — and is now `el_agg_arg_reaches`
// (`ElTy.fits` + `el_norm_cast`), with `compute_agg_bodies` writing the cast.
// That admitted `isize` and nothing else. `bool` has no `el_norm_cast` at all
// (`el_norm_cast` is empty outside the numeric classes), so it is refused by the
// FIRST branch — the class one — both before and after, and its wording is the
// only part of this file's `.expected` that changed.
package test;

use logos.std.wql.wql;
use logos.mem.collections.vec;

struct Rec { pub k: i64, pub v: bool }

pub deem lows(src: &[Rec]) { from src s
      group by s.k
      aggregate t = min(s.v)
      select t : i64 }

fn main() -> i32 { return 0i32; }
